# TestFlight configuration for tools/build_release.sh --testflight --upload.
#
# Copy this file to `testflight.env` alongside it and fill in one of the two
# blocks below:
#
#     cp tools/testflight.env.example tools/testflight.env
#
# `testflight.env` is gitignored. Keep it that way — it names the account your
# builds are published under.
#
# Only uploading reads this file. Building a signed .ipa needs nothing from here:
# the Team ID it signs with lives in ios/Signing.local.xcconfig.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Option 1 — an Apple Account and an app-specific password.
#
# The password comes from appleid.apple.com -> Sign-In and Security -> App-
# Specific Passwords. Your Apple Account password will not work.
#
# `@keychain:NAME` reads it from the login keychain instead of storing it here,
# which is what keeps this file free of secrets. Create the item once, either in
# Keychain Access (File -> New Password Item, with NAME as the item name), or:
#
#     security add-generic-password -a "you@example.com" -s MIRRICH_APP_PASSWORD -w
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
MIRRICH_APPLE_ID="you@example.com"
MIRRICH_APP_PASSWORD="@keychain:MIRRICH_APP_PASSWORD"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Option 2 — an App Store Connect API key, which takes precedence over Option 1.
#
# No password and no keychain prompt, which is what makes it the better choice
# for an unattended upload. In App Store Connect -> Users and Access ->
# Integrations -> App Store Connect API, create a key with the App Manager role.
# Download the .p8 (offered exactly once) to:
#
#     ~/.appstoreconnect/private_keys/AuthKey_<KEY-ID>.p8
#
# The issuer is the UUID shown above the key list, and is the same for every key.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MIRRICH_API_KEY="ABCD1234EF"
# MIRRICH_API_ISSUER="00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000"
